private_system.narrative:
Private care supplements the public system. About 37 percent of people hold private insurance, which is commonly used for faster access to elective surgery, specialist consultations and diagnostics.{{1}} Private hospitals focus on elective and nonemergency care and are paid through out-of-pocket fees, insurance and Accident Compensation Corporation reimbursements, and nearly all aged residential care is delivered by the private sector.{{1}}

Southern Cross is the largest name in the market. Southern Cross Medical Care Society holds about 60 percent of the health insurance market by customer numbers, and in the year to 30 June 2026 it paid a record $1.8 billion in claims and returned to a $146.9 million surplus after two years of deficits.{{2}} Southern Cross Healthcare describes itself as New Zealand's largest independent healthcare network, with wholly owned and joint venture hospitals; its hospitals and endoscopy clinics cared for more than 118,000 patients in the year to June 2025.{{3}} Evolution Healthcare runs six private hospitals, including Bowen and Wakefield in Wellington, Royston in Hastings, Grace in Tauranga and Anglesea in Hamilton, along with day surgery centers and the Birthcare maternity hospital in Auckland.{{4}} Australian investor QIC agreed to buy it from Pacific Equity Partners in December 2021.{{6}}

Government policy is drawing the sector in. In March 2025 the Health Minister directed Health New Zealand to offer private providers contracts of up to 10 years for elective surgery, and in July 2025 he announced an additional 21,000 elective procedures over the following year through an "Elective Boost" program.{{5}} In 2026 Health New Zealand signed its first long-term contracts with private hospitals, worth about $300 million over six years, with Southern Cross Healthcare, The Ascot Hospital and Clinic (Allevia) and Mercy Hospital Dunedin, to treat orthopaedic backlogs.{{7}}

education.narrative:
Medical education is concentrated at the University of Otago and the University of Auckland, and annual tuition ranges from NZ$20,000 to NZ$40,000.{{1}} Auckland's Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery takes six years of full-time study.{{2}} Graduates then complete two years of prevocational training as interns (postgraduate years 1 and 2), with four accredited clinical attachments in each year; doctors who pass the NZREX Clinical examination follow the same training.{{3}}

A third medical school is under construction. In July 2025 the government agreed to establish a medical school at the University of Waikato focused on primary care and rural and provincial practice, committing $82.85 million alongside a $151.86 million contribution from the university.{{4}} It will train 120 additional doctors a year, lifting total medical school places from 639 a year in 2026 to 759 from 2028.{{4}} Construction of its Hamilton building is scheduled for completion in December 2027, ahead of the first cohort in 2028.{{5}}

Overseas-trained doctors have faster routes in. In July 2025 the Medical Council introduced fast-track registration for GPs trained in the United States, Canada and Singapore, processed within two months, and added Chile, Luxembourg and Croatia to its comparable health system pathway, bringing the list to 29 countries.{{6}}
